<text><loc_264><loc_269><loc_456><loc_325>It will be noticed that the assumption in Theorem 1 that [E]>0 was only made in order to justify the analogue of (4). It is not necessary for the truth of (4) that |O|>0. For example, if C is the set of all real numbers in [0,1] which have decimal expansions (in scale 10) which miss the integer 5, it is easy to prove that C is closed and null. If now z=0,0,0,0... is any number in (0,1), it is the difference between two numbers in C, e. g.</text>
